      {
        q: "Can I bring ice packs, hand warmers, and distilled water on a plane?",
        a: "Gel ice packs must be completely frozen solid when presented at the security checkpoint to be allowed in carry-on bags — if they are partially melted or liquid, they are subject to the 3-1-1 rule. Hard-sided ice packs and plastic-wrapped dry ice (under 5.5 lbs) are permitted in checked bags. Hand warmers are allowed in carry-on and checked bags. Distilled water for CPAP machines is allowed in carry-on over 3.4 oz when declared as medically necessary."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "grooming-devices",
    label: "Beard Trimmers & Grooming Devices",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring a beard trimmer or hair trimmer on a plane?",
        a: "Yes — beard trimmers, hair trimmers, and electric clippers are allowed in both carry-on and checked bags. TSA does not restrict electric grooming devices. In carry-on, pack them accessibly so TSA can inspect them if needed. In checked luggage, they travel without issue. For international flights, note that some countries (like Japan or Australia) have no specific restrictions on grooming devices."
      },
      {
        q: "Should I put my trimmer in carry-on or checked baggage?",
        a: "You can bring a trimmer in either your carry-on or checked bag. Carry-on is perfectly fine — a beard trimmer is not a restricted item. If your trimmer has a lithium rechargeable battery that cannot be removed, it is actually safer to keep it in carry-on, since devices with built-in lithium batteries are technically preferred in cabin baggage per FAA guidelines. If your trimmer uses removable AA/AAA batteries, you can pack it in either bag."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring an electric shaver with a rechargeable battery in my carry-on or checked bag?",
        a: "Yes. Electric shavers and rechargeable electric razors are allowed in carry-on and checked bags. Shavers with built-in lithium-ion batteries (non-removable) are allowed in both — though FAA guidance generally prefers lithium devices in cabin. Shavers that use AA/AAA batteries can go anywhere. Brands like Philips Norelco, Braun, Panasonic shavers, and Gillette trimmers are all TSA-compliant in carry-on."
      },
      {
        q: "Are hair clippers, beard trimmers allowed in cabin (carry-on) baggage on international flights?",
        a: "Yes. Beard trimmers and hair clippers are universally allowed in cabin baggage on international flights, including on airlines like Ryanair, British Airways, United, Emirates, and Singapore Airlines. There are no international aviation rules banning grooming devices. If your trimmer is rechargeable, keeping it in the cabin is ideal for fire safety compliance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "camping-outdoor",
    label: "Camping Gear & Outdoor Equipment",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring tent stakes on a plane? Are they allowed in carry-on or checked bags?",
        a: "Tent stakes (tent pegs) are NOT allowed in carry-on bags because their pointed metal tips are classified as sharp objects that could be used as weapons. They must be packed in your checked baggage. This is the official TSA rule — tent stakes, tent pegs, and similar sharp camping items are checked-bag-only. In checked bags, they are fully permitted with no quantity restrictions."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring a tent on a plane as carry-on or checked luggage?",
        a: "Yes, tents are allowed on planes but must typically be checked due to size. Ultralight backpacking tents that pack small enough (under 22×14×9 inches) can go in carry-on. However, tent poles (if metal and sharp-tipped) must be checked; tent stakes must also be in checked baggage. The tent fabric and body can travel in either bag — it's the sharp metal accessories that must be checked."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring trekking poles or hiking poles on a plane?",
        a: "Hiking poles and trekking poles are NOT allowed in carry-on bags — they must be packed in checked baggage because their pointed tips could be used as weapons. This applies to all airlines. If you're on a minimalist trip without checked bags, you'll need to ship your poles or purchase them at your destination. Walking canes needed for medical/mobility purposes are allowed in the cabin."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring propane canisters or camping gas on a plane?",
        a: "No — propane canisters, butane canisters, and camping gas cartridges are strictly prohibited on commercial passenger aircraft in both carry-on and checked bags. They are classified as flammable compressed gases and are entirely banned by FAA, IATA, and TSA. Even empty canisters may be prohibited unless completely emptied and vented. You must purchase camping fuel at your destination."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "sports-fitness",
    label: "Sports Equipment & Fitness",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring dumbbells on a plane in carry-on or checked luggage?",
        a: "Dumbbells are generally allowed in checked bags, but NOT typically allowed in carry-on bags. TSA considers heavy, hard dumbbells as items that could be used as weapons. Even if TSA's own 'What Can I Bring' tool shows some allowance, individual TSA officers have discretion and will usually require heavy dumbbells to be checked. Rubber or lightweight dumbbells may occasionally be allowed at the officer's discretion, but checking them is the only guaranteed option."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring weights or weight plates on a plane?",
        a: "Weight plates, kettlebells, and heavy fitness weights are allowed in checked baggage but prohibited from carry-on due to their weight and potential use as weapons. Note that most airlines also have weight limits for checked bags (typically 50 lbs / 23 kg per bag), so you'll need to account for the added weight of your fitness equipment. Resistance bands, fitness mats, and jump ropes can go in carry-on bags without restrictions."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I take boxing gloves, a skipping rope, or gym equipment on a plane?",
        a: "Boxing gloves are allowed in both carry-on and checked bags — they are soft and not considered a security threat. Skipping ropes can go in carry-on unless the handles are made of very dense heavy material. Yoga mats can travel carry-on (rolled outside the bag) or in checked luggage. Most soft gym accessories (resistance bands, yoga blocks, gym gloves) are fully permitted in carry-on."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "food-snacks",
    label: "Snacks, Fruits & Specific Foods",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring protein bars, granola bars, and energy bars on a plane?",
        a: "Yes — protein bars, granola bars, energy bars, and nutrition bars are solid foods that are completely unrestricted by TSA in carry-on and checked bags. You can bring as many as you like. They are treated exactly like other solid snack foods (chips, crackers, jerky). No quantity limits apply. Protein bars from brands like Quest, KIND, Clif, and RX Bar are all welcome on board."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring bananas and fresh fruit through TSA and on a plane?",
        a: "Yes — bananas and most fresh fruits are allowed through TSA security checkpoints and in both carry-on and checked bags for domestic US flights. TSA does not restrict fresh fruit. However, for international flights, customs rules may prohibit bringing fresh fruit across borders: Australia has strict biosecurity rules against fresh produce; Canada and Japan also have restrictions on certain fruits. Always declare fresh fruit on customs forms for international travel."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring chips, potato chips, and bags of snacks on a plane?",
        a: "Yes — bags of chips, potato chips, tortilla chips, pretzels, popcorn, and similar snacks are fully allowed in carry-on and checked bags with no restrictions. These are solid dry foods and do not fall under the TSA 3-1-1 liquids rule. You can bring opened or unopened bags. Airport security X-ray machines will scan them but they are not flagged as restricted items."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring dog food, wet dog food, or dog treats on a plane?",
        a: "Dry dog food, dry dog treats, and dog biscuits are solid foods allowed in carry-on and checked bags with no restrictions. Wet or canned dog food is considered a gel/paste and falls under the TSA 3-1-1 liquids rule for carry-on bags — containers must be 3.4 oz or less. Larger cans of wet dog food must go in your checked baggage. For international flights, customs agencies may confiscate animal products — check destination country rules."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring coffee beans on a plane in carry-on or checked bags?",
        a: "Yes — coffee beans (whole bean, ground coffee, or instant coffee) are allowed in carry-on and checked bags. TSA treats coffee as a solid dry food. Loose coffee grounds over 12 oz may be subject to additional X-ray screening at some checkpoints. Pre-packaged sealed bags of coffee are easier to screen. Coffee beans in carry-on bags are fully TSA-compliant with no restrictions."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring carbonated drinks (canned soda, sparkling water) on a plane?",
        a: "Sealed cans or bottles of carbonated drinks (Coke, Pepsi, sparkling water, energy drinks) count as liquids and must follow the TSA 3-1-1 rule in carry-on bags — containers must be 3.4 oz (100ml) or less. Standard 12 oz cans of soda are too large for carry-on. However, carbonated drinks in any quantity can be packed in your checked bags. You can buy carbonated beverages after passing through security at airport stores."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring honey to Canada? Is honey allowed on international flights?",
        a: "Yes, you can bring honey to Canada. The Canadian Food Inspection Agency (CFIA) allows passengers to bring commercially packaged honey into Canada for personal use. Honey that is sealed, labeled, and commercially produced generally passes Canadian customs without issue. However, large quantities may need to be declared. Honey in carry-on bags must comply with the TSA/airport 3-1-1 liquids rule — containers must be 3.4 oz (100ml) or smaller; larger jars must go in checked bags."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "shoes-accessories",
    label: "Shoes, Jewelry & Accessories",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring high heels in my carry-on bag?",
        a: "Yes — high heels and other footwear are allowed in carry-on bags with no TSA restrictions. Shoes are not prohibited items. You can pack heels, sandals, sneakers, and boots in your carry-on. Note that large heels take up significant space, so consider checking them if bag space is limited. TSA agents may ask you to remove shoes from your bag for X-ray screening at some checkpoints."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I pack a smartwatch in checked luggage?",
        a: "Smartwatches (Apple Watch, Samsung Galaxy Watch, Fitbit, Garmin) can technically be packed in checked luggage, but it is strongly not recommended. Smartwatches contain lithium batteries, and while the FAA primarily restricts spare/loose lithium batteries in checked bags, it is best practice to keep devices with lithium batteries in carry-on. Also, valuable electronics in checked bags risk theft or damage from baggage handling."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring a watch in checked baggage?",
        a: "Analog watches without batteries, or watches with small coin cell batteries, can go in either checked or carry-on baggage. Smartwatches with lithium-ion batteries are best kept in carry-on. For valuable or luxury watches, always carry them in your personal item or carry-on to prevent loss, theft, or damage in checked bags. Jewelry and watches do not need to be declared unless they are high-value items for customs purposes."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "tools-hardware",
    label: "Tools, Screwdrivers & Hardware",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring a screwdriver on a plane?",
        a: "Screwdrivers with a shaft 7 inches or shorter (measured from the tip to where the handle begins) are allowed in carry-on bags. Screwdrivers longer than 7 inches must be in checked bags. This is the official TSA rule for tools. Most standard household screwdrivers are typically 6–8 inches, so measure yours before packing. All screwdrivers, regardless of size, are allowed in checked baggage."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring a box cutter on a plane? Can it go in checked luggage?",
        a: "Box cutters and utility knives with retractable blades are NOT allowed in carry-on bags. They must be packed in your checked baggage. In checked bags, box cutters are fully permitted — just make sure to sheath or wrap the blade to protect baggage handlers. Snap-off blade box cutters and Stanley-type utility knives are all restricted to checked bags only."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring matches or a matchbook on a plane?",
        a: "One book of safety matches is allowed in carry-on bags or on your person. Strike-anywhere matches are completely prohibited on all flights, in both carry-on and checked bags. You cannot pack any matches in your checked luggage — only in carry-on or on your person (one book limit per passenger). Safety matches (the kind that only light on the box) are the only permitted type."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring a hammer or other tools on a plane?",
        a: "Tools like hammers, wrenches, pliers, and similar items are allowed in carry-on bags only if they are 7 inches or shorter. Tools longer than 7 inches — including most hammers — must go in checked baggage. Hammers are generally considered checked-bag items since even a small hammer could be used as a weapon. All tools are permitted in checked bags without restriction."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "liquids-special",
    label: "Contact Solution, Special Liquids & Gases",
    faqs: [
      {
        q: "Can I bring contact lens solution on a plane?",
        a: "Yes. Contact lens solution is allowed in carry-on bags. While it is technically a liquid and subject to the 3-1-1 rule (3.4 oz / 100ml per container), contact solution is generally considered a medical necessity. TSA allows contact solution in larger quantities when it is declared as medically necessary at the checkpoint. Standard travel-sized contact solution (up to 3.4 oz) is fully TSA-compliant without any declaration. Larger bottles should go in checked bags or be declared at screening."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring candles on a plane — wax candles, scented candles?",
        a: "Solid wax candles (pillar candles, taper candles, scented solid candles) are allowed in both carry-on and checked bags with no restrictions. Gel candles (containing liquid gel) are considered a liquid and must comply with the TSA 3-1-1 rule in carry-on (3.4 oz / 100ml or less). Candles with very strong scents may be flagged by security for additional inspection, but are not prohibited. Wax melts and solid fragrance bars are also permitted."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring a hookah on a plane?",
        a: "Hookah pipes (shisha pipes) are allowed in checked baggage. A disassembled hookah with all parts packed securely in your checked bag is permitted. Hookah charcoal (solid charcoal) is also allowed in checked bags. Hookah tobacco/shisha is allowed in checked and carry-on bags. Hookah components that contain liquids or gel must follow the 3-1-1 rule in carry-on. Hookah accessories in carry-on may be subject to additional inspection. Large glass hookah bases risk breakage in checked bags — pack with care."
      },
      {
        q: "Can I bring carbonated beverages in checked luggage?",
        a: "Yes — carbonated drinks like canned soda, sparkling water, and carbonated beverages can be packed in your checked luggage without TSA restrictions. Cabin pressure changes and turbulence can rarely cause carbonated cans to leak, so place them in a sealed plastic bag inside your checked bag. Opened carbonated beverages may fizz, so seal them tightly. There is no TSA restriction on the quantity of carbonated drinks in checked bags."
      }
    ]
  }
];

// Build the flat FAQ list for JSON-LD (all questions across all categories)
const allFaqs = categories.flatMap(cat => cat.faqs);

const currentUrl = "https://bringonplane.com/faq";

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://bringonplane.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "FAQ", "item": currentUrl }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": allFaqs.map(faq => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": faq.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": faq.a
    }
  }))
};
---

<Layout
  title="TSA FAQ: What Can I Bring on a Plane? Baggage Rules for 2026"
  description="Complete FAQ: can I bring a beard trimmer, tent stakes, dumbbells, matches, protein bars, honey to Canada, contact solution, candles, box cutters, electric shaver, dog food, and more on a plane? Updated 2026 TSA rules for carry-on and checked bags."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} slot="head" />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} slot="head" />
  <link rel="canonical" href={currentUrl} slot="head" />

  <Header slot="header" />

  <main class="flex-1">

    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="border-b border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
      <div class="max-w-[860px] mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-12">
        <div class="mb-6">
          <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs font-mono text-mute hover:text-ink transition-colors">
            <ArrowLeft class="w-3.5 h-3.5" />
            Back to Home
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3 mb-4">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-md bg-link-bg-soft text-link border border-link/20 flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5">
            <HelpCircle class="w-5 h-5" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-1.5px] text-ink leading-tight">
              What Can I Bring on a Plane?
            </h1>
            <p class="text-sm text-body leading-[1.7] mt-2 max-w-[600px]">
              Official TSA rules for food, liquids, electronics, medications, vapes, razors, toiletries, and more. Updated for 2026 travel season.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Quick stat strip -->
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-6">
          {categories.map(cat => (
            <a
              href={`#cat-${cat.id}`}
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong bg-canvas hover:bg-canvas-soft text-mute hover:text-ink transition-all"
            >
              {cat.label}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Main content -->
    <div class="max-w-[860px] mx-auto px-4 md:px-6 py-14 space-y-16">

      {categories.map((cat, catIdx) => (
        <section id={`cat-${cat.id}`} class="scroll-mt-20">
          <!-- Category header -->
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
            <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-sm bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
              <Package class="w-4 h-4 text-mute" />
            </div>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.5px] text-ink">{cat.label}</h2>
            <span class="text-[10px] font-mono text-mute border border-hairline px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded ml-auto flex-shrink-0">
              {cat.faqs.length} questions
            </span>
          </div>

          <!-- Accordion items -->
          <div class="space-y-3">
            {cat.faqs.map((faq, idx) => {
              const faqId = `faq-${cat.id}-${idx}`;
              return (
                <div class="border border-hairline rounded-md bg-canvas shadow-sm overflow-hidden">
                  <button
                    type="button"
                    class="faq-toggle-btn w-full text-left px-5 py-4 flex items-start justify-between gap-4 text-sm font-medium text-ink hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors cursor-pointer outline-none select-none"
                    data-faq-target={faqId}
                    aria-expanded="false"
                    aria-controls={faqId}
                  >
                    <span class="leading-snug pt-0.5">{faq.q}</span>
                    <ChevronDown class="w-4 h-4 text-mute transition-transform duration-200 flex-shrink-0 mt-0.5" />
                  </button>
                  <div
                    id={faqId}
                    class="faq-body hidden border-t border-hairline/50 px-5 py-4 text-xs text-body leading-[1.8] bg-canvas-soft/40"
                    role="region"
                  >
                    {faq.a}
                  </div>
                </div>
              );
            })}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}

      <!-- Bottom CTA -->
      <div class="border border-hairline rounded-md p-8 text-center bg-canvas-soft">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.5px] text-ink mb-2">Check Any Item Instantly</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-body mb-5 max-w-[400px] mx-auto leading-[1.6]">
          Search our full database of 100+ items to get TSA carry-on and checked bag verdicts instantly.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
          <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-link hover:text-link-deep transition-colors border border-link/30 hover:border-link px-4 py-2.5 rounded-md bg-link-bg-soft">
            Search Items →
          </a>
          <a href="/packing-assistant" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs font-medium text-ink hover:text-link transition-colors border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong px-4 py-2.5 rounded-md">
            Use Packing Assistant →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>

<script>
  function setupFaqToggles() {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(".faq-toggle-btn").forEach(btn => {
      btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const targetId = btn.getAttribute("data-faq-target") ?? "";
        const body = document.getElementById(targetId);
        const icon = btn.querySelector("svg");
        const isExpanded = btn.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true";

        if (body) {
          if (isExpanded) {
            body.classList.add("hidden");
            btn.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
            icon?.classList.remove("rotate-180");
          } else {
            body.classList.remove("hidden");
            btn.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
            icon?.classList.add("rotate-180");
          }
        }
      });
    });
  }

  setupFaqToggles();
  document.addEventListener("astro:after-swap", setupFaqToggles);
</script>
